[[File:1971 Buick Riviera (15117991390).jpg|thumb|left|1971 Buick Riviera]]
[[File:1972 Buick Riviera (37289777492).jpg|left|thumb|1972 Buick Riviera rear]]
[[File:1973 Buick Riviera (24375794184).jpg|left|thumb|1973 Buick Riviera]]
[[File:1971 Buick Riviera coupe (8706820216).jpg|thumb|left|1971 Buick Riviera interior]]
[[File:Buick Riviera 1971 engine.jpg|thumb|left|V8 engine of a 1971 Buick Riviera]]

The Riviera was radically redesigned for the 1971 model year with flowing and dramatic "boat-tail" styling.<ref>{{cite web |title=buick-riviera-1973a.jpg|work=Muscle Car Club|url=http://www.musclecarclub.com/musclecars/buick-riviera/images/buick-riviera-1973a.jpg |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20030124110238/http://musclecarclub.com/musclecars/buick-riviera/images/buick-riviera-1973a.jpg|archive-date=2003-01-24|url-status=dead}}</ref> Designed under [[Bill Mitchell (automobile designer)|Bill Mitchell's]] direction, it was penned by [[Jerry Hirshberg]], future head of design for [[Nissan]], mating the two-piece [[Custom car#Language|vee-butted]]{{sfnp|Flory|2004|p=792}} [[fastback]] rear window, inspired by the 1963 [[Chevrolet Corvette (C2)|Corvette Sting Ray]] coupe, to the Riviera's platform.

The design was originally intended for the smaller [[GM A platform|A-body]] or its related [[GM G platform|G-body]], as shown by a full scale clay model of an A-body based boat-tail Riviera recently revealed.<ref name=Deans>{{cite web |url=http://www.deansgarage.com/2020/1971-boat-tail-riviera-a-body/ |title=1971 Boat-Tail Riviera A-body |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201130131251/http://www.deansgarage.com/2020/1971-boat-tail-riviera-a-body/ |archive-date=2020-11-30 |first=Bruce |last=Brooks |work=Dean's Garage |publisher=Performance Design |date=2020-06-09}}</ref> Given the late stage of the 1968-72 A/G platform evolution and accretive cost to add another version to it, GM Management decreed that the next Riviera use the full sized [[GM B platform]] body&mdash;expanded for 1971 by {{cvt|3|in|mm}} in wheelbase and more than {{cvt|120|lb|kg}} heavier&mdash; which produced controversial looks, making for a sharp departure from those of the Toronado and Eldorado. (''Collectible Automobile'' ran an article about 1971–76 full-sized Buicks in which one sketch design for their 2-door coupes which was rejected resembled the 1971–73 Riviera).

This generation introduced a much more visual representation of the "[[Buick#Sweepspear|sweepspear]]", with a more faithful representation to the version that appeared on 1950s Buicks in both the side molding and [[Beltline (automotive)|beltline]].{{sfnp|Savage|1993|p=54}} Large, round wheel openings were intended to convey more of a sporty air.{{sfnp|Savage|1993|p=54}}{{Clarify|reason=what does it mean by "more sporty air"? that language is confusing for most readers, including me, is there a better word that could be used?|date=January 2022}} The only engine available was Buick's own [[Buick V8 engine#455|455&nbsp;ci V8 engine]] producing {{cvt|315|hp|kW|0}}, with {{cvt|330|hp|kW|0}} with the Gran Sport (GS) package.{{sfnp|Savage|1993|p=63}}

The 455 engine had a lower [[compression ratio]] to meet [[United States Environmental Protection Agency|EPA]] emissions requirements, together with the shift from [[Horsepower#SAE gross power|SAE gross]] to [[Horsepower#SAE net power|SAE net]] ratings this reduced claimed power to {{cvt|255|hp|kW|0}}, with {{cvt|265|hp|kW|0}} in the Gran Sport. Performance remained reasonably brisk, with a 0–60&nbsp;mph time of 8.1 seconds for the GS, but the Riviera's sporty image was rapidly fading.{{According to whom|date=January 2022}} One noteworthy advance was Buick's '''Max Trac''' [[Traction control system|traction control]].<ref>{{cite web |url=http://www.oldcarbrochures.com/static/NA/Buick/1971_Buick/1971_Buick_Riviera_Brochure/1971%20Buick%20Riviera%20Brochure-05.html |title=1971 Buick Riviera Brochure |page=5 |website=Oldcarbrochures.com |access-date=2012-11-08}}</ref> The 1971 Riviera also features GM's "Full-Flo" ventilation system and two large deck lid louvers are prominent on the trunk lid.

Despite these features, Riviera sales for 1971 dropped to 33,810,{{sfnp|Flory|2004|p=798}} the lowest to date. The 1972 Riviera received a new, egg-crate grille, more substantial front bumpers to prepare for the new [[Bumper (automobile)#5mph|5-mph impact legislation]], restyled taillight trim, and the louvers were removed from the trunk lid. The 1972 Riviera also featured a redesigned ventilation system, and the 455 engine switched to net power ratings, {{cvt|225|hp|kW|0}} or {{cvt|250|hp|kW|0}} in the Gran Sport, although the actual drop in net power was only {{cvt|5|hp|kW|0}}. Sales remained stagnant at 33,728, a drop of 82 from the prior year.{{sfnp|Flory|2004|p=876}}

For 1973, the Riviera underwent a number of changes.{{sfnp|Savage|1993|p=60}} The front bumper was redesigned to be thicker and featured bumper guards as standard in order to meet 1974 impact-bumper standards, the grille was switched back to horizontal slats, and the front lamps were moved from the bumper and were now integrated into the headlights, wrapping around the corners of the car. Sluggish sales of the third generation Riviera led GM to believe that the boattail deck lid was too radical for most customers' tastes, so in 1973 it was blunted and made slightly shorter. The taillights, meanwhile, were moved down from the sheet metal and into the bumper, and the rear license plate location was moved from the left side of the bumper to the center.{{sfnp|Savage|1993|p=60}} The {{cvt|250|hp|kW|0}} engine became standard, with {{cvt|260|hp|kW|0}} with the '''Stage One''' package. This also included a limited-slip [[Differential (mechanical device)|differential]] and a chrome-plated air cleaner. The "Gran Sport" package was still available as a separate option package consisting of a ride-and-handling package that included a rear stabilizer bar, JR78-15 whitewall steel-belted radial tires, a specially tuned "radial roadability" suspension, additional sound insulation and special "Gran Sport" badging. The design changes however only led to a marginal increase in sales, with 34,080 being produced for the model year.

