[[File:BuickRivera.jpg|thumb|left|Buick Riviera coupe]]
Buick presented the 1979 model year [[front wheel drive]] Riviera, its first front-drive production model. Built on a {{cvt|114|in|mm}} wheelbase, it once again shared its mechanical design and [[GM E platform|platform]] with the [[Cadillac Eldorado]] and [[Oldsmobile Toronado]]. The Olds 403 and Buick 350 were dropped, but the Olds 350 remained, as did a new [[turbocharger|turbocharged]] [[Buick V6 engine]] of {{cvt|231|cuin|L|1}} displacement with {{cvt|185|hp|kW|0}} which was installed in the Riviera S-Type, shared with the [[Buick Regal#Second generation (1978)|Regal Sport Coupe]] turbo for the 1980 model year.{{sfnp|Flammang|1999|p=}} The Riviera became ''[[Motor Trend]]''{{'s}} [[Car of the Year]]. Sales more than doubled, to 52,181 for 1979 and 48,621 for the similar 1980 models.<ref name="Evolution"/>

[[File:1983 Buick Riviera XX, front right (Hershey 2019).jpg|thumb|left|The 1983 Buick Riviera XX special edition]]
[[File:1984 Riviera Convertible.jpg|thumb|left|Rear view of 1984 Riviera convertible]]
1981 saw the Turbo renamed '''[[T-Type]]''' and the demise of the 350 engine in favor of the [[Oldsmobile V8 engine|Oldsmobile-built {{cvt|307|cuin|L|1}}]] with {{cvt|140|hp|kW|0}} (phased in during the 1980 MY). The standard engine was now Buick's {{cvt|125|hp|kW|0}} {{cvt|252|cuin|L|1}} [[V6]], and a new option was an Oldsmobile [[diesel engine]] with a mere {{cvt|105|hp|kW|0}} offered through 1985. 1982 also saw the first-ever Riviera [[convertible]], although relatively few were built, owing to a high price - US$23,944 (${{formatnum:{{Inflation|US|23944|1982}}}} in {{Inflation-year|US}} dollars {{inflation-fn|US}}).{{sfnp|Flammang|1999|p=}} The Riviera convertible was available in white or red [[Metallic paint#Fire-Frost and Firemist|Firemist]] with red leather. A [[:File:Indypacecar1983.jpg|twin-turbocharged Riviera convertible]] was chosen to be the pace car at the 1983 [[Indianapolis 500]], tuned to produce {{cvt|410|hp|kW|0}}.<ref name=specialed>{{cite magazine |title=Special Edition Coupes – 1983 Buick Riviera, 1985 Ford Thunderbird |url=https://www.hemmings.com/blog/article/special-edition-coupes-1983-buick-riviera-1985-ford-thunderbird/ |magazine=Hemmings |last=Traver Adolphus |first=David |date=September 2012 |publisher=American City Business Journals}}</ref> Most convertible Rivieras had the V8 engine, which saw an increase in rated SAE net HP to 150 for both convertibles and coupes fitted with it from 1982 through the 1985 model year.

In 1983,  a special edition of 500 "Riviera XX" was offered, celebrating twenty years since the introduction of the first Riviera (502 were built in the end). These have a special two-tone exterior paint, real wire wheels, a leather, and walnut interior as well as [[Fineness#Karat|24-karat]] gold plated "Riviera XX" badging.<ref name=specialed/> The Riviera XX also received a special grille, which then became part of the 1984 model year facelift model.<ref name=specialed/> Overall sales made the 1980s Riviera a great success, reaching 65,305 for the 1985 model year.<ref name="production"/>

