[[File:Buick-TouchScreen-x.jpg|thumb|left|Buick Graphic Control]]
The [[GM E platform|E-body]] coupes were converted to [[Monocoque|unibody construction]] and transverse front-wheel drive &mdash; and was further downsized for 1986, to a {{cvt|108|in|mm|0}} wheelbase. The V6 was now the only engine, fitted with sequential fuel injection (SFI) and was rated initially at {{cvt|140|hp|kW|0}} SAE ([[Buick V6 engine#LG2|LG2]]) and {{cvt|200|lbft|Nm|0}} of torque. It used the Turbo-[[Hydramatic]] 440-T4 [[automatic transmission]] with a 2.84:1 final drive ratio. The 3.8 was the only engine used for the seventh generation Riviera, although its power climbed incrementally over the years. For 1987, the engine was changed to the [[Buick V6 engine#LG3|LG3]] with roller lifters rather than flat lifters, power increased to {{cvt|150|hp|kW|0}} SAE while the torque remained unchanged. This was replaced by the [[Buick V6 engine#LN3|LN3]], with power and torque up to {{cvt|165|hp|kW|0}} and {{cvt|210|lbft|Nm|0}} respectively thanks to multi-port fuel injection, balance shafts, and other improvements. The LN3 was used from 1988 until 1990. For the last three model years Buick used the [[Buick V6 engine#L27|L27]] iteration with sequential, tuned port fuel injection (TPI), which gained a few more horsepower and more mid-range torque from a new, two-piece intake manifold with longer runners. Power crept up to {{cvt|170|hp|kW|0}} at 4,800&nbsp;rpm, torque was up to {{cvt|220|lbft|Nm|0}} at 3,200&nbsp;rpm.
[[File:1987 Buick Riviera in Silver, rear left side.jpg|left|thumb|1986–1989 Riviera rear (pre-facelift)]]
This generation featured the ''Graphic Control Center'' (GCC), an advanced electronic instrumentation system which employed a dash-mounted {{convert|9|in|mm|adj=on}} monochrome touchscreen [[Cathode-ray tube|CRT]] display. The GCC controlled the vehicle's climate control system and stereo, and also supplied advanced instrumentation such as a [[trip computer]], vehicle diagnostics, and a maintenance reminder feature. Four-wheel disc brakes were standard. With a choice of three suspension packages available, up to the performance-oriented FE3 setting, handling was notably improved. The Riviera placed fourth for ''[[Motor Trend]]''{{'}}s 1986 Car of the Year contest. 

The seventh generation Riviera saw the installation of Dynaride, which was an air compressor that would pressurize the rear [[Chapman strut]]s to maintain a level overall ride height. A badge was installed on the dashboard to the left of the steering column on all vehicles equipped. It was not available on vehicles equipped with Gran Touring Suspension.

[[File:Buick Riviera -- 07-23-2009.jpg|thumb|left|1989–1993 Riviera (facelift)]]

[[File:89 Buick Riviera (8731450251).jpg|thumb|left|1989–1993 Riviera rear (facelift)]]

Fuel economy was notably improved for the 1986 Riviera. Prices increase to $19,831 (${{formatnum:{{Inflation|US|19831|1986}}}} in {{Inflation-year|US}} dollars{{inflation-fn|US}}) for the base model and $21,577 for the [[T-Type]] (${{formatnum:{{Inflation|US|21577|1986}}}} in {{Inflation-year|US}} dollars{{inflation-fn|US}}). Sales declined to 22,138 for 1986, 15,223 for 1987, and 8,625 for 1988. 1988 also saw the introduction of Buick's [[Buick Reatta|Reatta]] coupe, a two-seat personal luxury car.

The Riviera was restyled for 1989, adding {{convert|11|in|mm}} to the overall length (on an unchanged wheelbase). Sales rose to 21,189 for 1989, but dropped to a low of 4,555 for 1993, the abbreviated final model year of this generation.<ref name="production"/> 

