After a hiatus for the 1994 model year, the Riviera returned for the 1995 model year with a complete redesign. The {{cvt|205|hp|kW|0}} [[naturally aspirated]] 3800 V6 was the standard engine, with an optional [[supercharger|supercharged]] 3800 V6 rated at {{cvt|225|hp|kW|0}} and {{cvt|275|lbft|Nm|0}}. The eighth generation was manufactured in [[Orion Township, Michigan]], using the same Cadillac-derived [[GM G platform (FWD)|G platform]] as the 4-door [[Oldsmobile Aurora]]. The first of 41,422 1995 Rivieras was manufactured on May 23, 1994.{{cn|date=November 2023}}

In 1996, the supercharged engine's output increased to {{cvt|240|hp|kW|0}} and {{cvt|280|lbft|Nm|0}}, paired with a 4T60E-HD transmission. 18,036 Rivieras were manufactured in 1996.

For the 1997 model year, engineers revised the suspension revisions, removing weight. An upgraded [[GM 4T65E-HD transmission#4T65E-HD|4T65E-HD transmission]] featuring a larger {{cvt|258|mm|in|1|order=flip}} [[torque converter]] and heavy-duty [[gearbox]] were added. 18,827 were made in 1997.
[[File:1999 Buick Riviera 3.8L, rear 8.25.19.jpg|left|thumb|1999 Buick Riviera rear ]]
For 1998, the {{cvt|240|hp|kW|0}} supercharged V6 became standard. GM's [[OnStar]] service was added as an option, along with minor interior design changes and features including new passenger heated seats and the removal of the front "55/45" 3-seater split bench option. A total of 10,953 units were produced for 1998.

With sales of all coupes declining in the North American market, GM discontinued the Riviera. 1999 was the car's last model year with production of 1,956 cars ending on November 25, 1998. The final 200 cars, marketed as ''Silver Arrow'' models, carried special silver paint and trim,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.rivowners.org/silver.html |title=Special Report |work=Riviera Owners Association |access-date=12 November 2019}}</ref> recalling several earlier ''Silver Arrow'' concept cars using the earliest Riviera by [[Bill Mitchell (designer)|Bill Mitchell]].

The Eighth-generation Riviera's supercharged [[Overhead valve|OHV]] V6 enabled a {{convert|0|-|60|mph|km/h}} time of under 7 seconds and a standing {{convert|1/4|mi|m}} in 15.5 seconds With EPA mileage estimates of {{cvt|18|mpgus}} / {{cvt|27|mpgus}} (city/highway).

